            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Francis I</strong><br>\nFrancis I was born on September 12, 1494, in the Château de Cognac in France. The only son of Charles, Count of Angoulême, and Louise of Savoy, he was a member of the Valois-Angoulême branch of the Valois dynasty. Raised by his mother after his father's death, Francis received a comprehensive education in languages, the arts, and military tactics, preparing him for future leadership.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Accession to the Throne</strong><br>\nIn 1515, Francis I ascended to the throne after the death of his cousin, King Louis XII, who left no male heir. His reign began with an ambitious military campaign in Italy, where he sought to assert his claim over the Duchy of Milan. His victory at the Battle of Marignano allowed him to secure the duchy, establishing him as a prominent monarch in European politics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Patron of the Arts</strong><br>\nFrancis I is often remembered for his patronage of the arts and his role in initiating the French Renaissance. He invited Leonardo da Vinci to his court and secured the Mona Lisa for France. He also began the construction of the grand Château de Chambord, reflecting his taste for Renaissance architecture. His reign brought about significant cultural growth and set France as a center of artistic innovation.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
