          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Francis I's Wars in Italy</strong><br>\nThe desire to control Italy led Francis I into a series of conflicts known as the Italian Wars, which pitted France against the Holy Roman Empire, Spain, and several Italian states. Despite initial successes, Francis suffered a devastating defeat at the Battle of Pavia in 1525, where he was captured by Imperial troops and held captive for more than a year.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Field of the Cloth of Gold</strong><br>\nIn 1520, Francis I met with Henry VIII of England in an event known as the Field of the Cloth of Gold. This meeting, characterized by lavish festivities and chivalric contests, was an attempt to strengthen the alliance between France and England. However, the extravagant spending did little to prevent future conflicts between the two nations.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legal and Administrative Reforms</strong><br>\nFrancis I implemented significant legal and administrative reforms that modernized the French government. He developed a more centralized bureaucracy by appointing new officials and creating specialized councils. His legal reforms included the Ordinance of Villers-Cotterêts, which mandated the use of French in all legal documents, diminishing the use of Latin and standardizing the French language.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
