          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Religious Turmoil and the Affair of the Placards</strong><br>\nReligious strife marked Francis I's reign when the Affair of the Placards in 1534 sparked tensions between Catholics and the growing number of French Protestants. The scandal involved anti-Catholic posters appearing overnight in major cities, including one on the king's bedroom door. This incident led Francis to take a harder stance against Protestantism, culminating in increased persecution.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Relations with the Ottoman Empire</strong><br>\nFrancis I pursued an unusual alliance with the Ottoman Empire, which was led by Sultan Suleiman the Magnificent. This was part of his strategy to counter the Habsburg threat from the Holy Roman Empire and Spain. Their collaboration included military cooperation and trade agreements, marking one of the first significant non-Christian alliances in European history.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nFrancis I died on March 31, 1547, leaving behind a legacy as a ruler who enhanced France's status as a European power and a cultural hub. His reign was marked by his support for education, the arts, and the development of a strong central government. His son, Henry II, succeeded him, continuing the policies that Francis had implemented during his rule.</p>\n"
              },
